<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">East African Rift</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Great Rift Valley</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The East African Rift is a system of rift valleys running from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/afar">Afar</a> region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ethiopia">Ethiopia</a> through Kenya to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mozambique">Mozambique</a>, part of what is often called the Great Rift Valley. Its floor holds a chain of lakes and fertile volcanic soils. In Ethiopia it separated highland kingdoms such as Wolaita from their neighbours, and in Kenya it divided the coast from the western highlands.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/east-african-rift.jpg" alt="East African Rift" width="320" height="270" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: USGS · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tectonical_map_of_East_Africa.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
